WEBVTT

00:00:01.290 --> 00:00:02.290
Hi, I'm Coaming!

00:00:02.290 --> 00:00:05.955
Standard cross-entropy has a gradient that can silently round to zero.

00:00:05.955 --> 00:00:07.954
Here is how, and the fix.

00:00:09.517 --> 00:00:12.923
The standard code adds a tiny number to one, and it vanishes.

00:00:12.923 --> 00:00:14.626
The fix never adds the one.

00:00:19.317 --> 00:00:23.101
Here m is the margin: how far the right logit sits above the others.

00:00:23.521 --> 00:00:26.942
The usual form adds e to the minus m to one.

00:00:26.942 --> 00:00:30.674
In float thirty-two, past a margin near sixteen point six, that sum

00:00:30.674 --> 00:00:32.540
is one, and the gradient zero.

00:00:32.960 --> 00:00:36.266
The fixed form keeps e to the minus m on top, accurate

00:00:36.266 --> 00:00:37.643
until the exponent runs out.

00:00:39.717 --> 00:00:44.498
After memorising, margins grow past the cliff, and the target gradients go to zero.

00:00:44.918 --> 00:00:49.142
With almost no gradient, Adam's running average of squared gradients decays.

00:00:49.562 --> 00:00:53.374
Weight decay pulls a few examples back, and their gradients switch on again.

00:00:53.794 --> 00:00:55.936
Adam divides them by that tiny average.

00:00:55.936 --> 00:00:58.995
The step is huge, the loss spikes, and it repeats.

00:01:00.483 --> 00:01:02.350
Bug two is in distillation.

00:01:02.350 --> 00:01:06.831
Student and teacher are rounded separately, then subtracted, so even a perfect

00:01:06.831 --> 00:01:08.325
match leaves a gradient.

00:01:08.745 --> 00:01:12.461
The fix starts from the logit gap, in log space, so equal

00:01:12.461 --> 00:01:13.699
logits give exactly zero.

00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:18.223
In the paper's runs, the standard margin saws up to the

00:01:18.223 --> 00:01:19.286
cliff and falls back.

00:01:19.706 --> 00:01:21.278
The fixed loss crosses smoothly.

00:01:23.425 --> 00:01:27.432
On a shared cloud CPU, standard cross-entropy slingshot six times in every

00:01:27.432 --> 00:01:29.102
seed, the fixed loss never.

00:01:29.102 --> 00:01:32.775
But in my runs the fix did not speed up grokking.

00:01:33.933 --> 00:01:37.358
If the number you need is tiny, compute it directly, never as

00:01:37.358 --> 00:01:39.071
one minus something close to one.

00:01:41.650 --> 00:01:45.886
A gradient that rounds to zero, Adam turning it into slingshots, and a rewrite.

00:01:46.306 --> 00:01:48.442
Every source is in the full article.

00:01:48.442 --> 00:01:49.052
I'm Coaming.

00:01:49.052 --> 00:01:49.357
Bye!
